{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB04572 - MONDO_20543",
  "verbalization": "Thiotepa, a chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the treatment of ovarian tumors through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Thiotepa may be linked to Theca Steroid-producing Cell Malignant Tumor Of Ovary, Not Further Specified via the intermediate cancers of Ovarian Carcinosarcoma and Malignant Dysgerminomatous Germ Cell Tumor Of Ovary. These cancers are both associated with the use of Thiotepa and other drugs like Carboplatin and Melphalan, which are also linked to the same target tumor. The shared pathway involves the use of chemotherapy agents that may exert cytotoxic effects on the tumor cells, potentially leading to the development or progression of Theca Steroid-producing Cell Malignant Tumor Of Ovary, Not Further Specified. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Thiotepa could contribute to the pathogenesis of this tumor, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
